menu "Dancefloor diagnostics"

    config DANCEFLOOR_LOG_PERIOD_S
        int "Seconds between periodic status lines"
        default 20
        range 5 300
        help
            How often the recurring status lines print: the hub's ring/phase
            line, the satellite's buffer/phase line, the SBC link summary, the
            visualiser's frame counts, and the TSF comparison.

            Logging only. Every servo, window and estimator keeps its own
            cadence -- the 5 s loops still run at 5 s and still see every
            sample, they simply do not narrate each pass -- so changing this
            cannot change behaviour.

            At 5 s the two boards emitted a line every 0.8 s between them, most
            of it identical to the line before. Anything eventful -- a retune, a
            splice, a track boundary, a decode error, dropped audio -- is logged
            when it happens and is not on this clock. Two of the lines above
            also print immediately on a bad window regardless of this setting,
            because waiting to hear about a fault is how faults get missed.

            Lives here because both firmwares require this component, and
            because Kconfig symbols are global in ESP-IDF: the LED component
            reads it without needing to depend on the sync protocol.

    config DANCEFLOOR_SBC_LINK_SPI_HZ
        int "Bridge -> hub SPI clock (Hz)"
        default 5000000
        range 100000 40000000
        help
            SCK frequency the bt_bridge master drives on the one-way SPI link to
            the hub. The slave is clocked by the master, so only the bridge
            build's value is on the wire; the hub build carries a copy only so
            its startup log prints the right number.

            1 MHz is where the link starts -- 20x the UART's 50 kB/s, against a
            measured ~42 kB/s stream. The breadboard jumpers this lives on were
            the UART's ceiling at 500 kbaud, and SPI adds a clock line to the
            bundle, so expect signal integrity, not the protocol, to be the
            limit. If the sbc_in crc/hdr counters move at one speed, drop this
            and reflash the BRIDGE; the hub need not match unless its log should.

            Default is 5 MHz: the 2048-payload frame (2060 bytes on the wire,
            sized for the codec's bitpool 250) is clean at 5 MHz but fails at
            10 MHz on these jumpers -- the hub's sbc_in reports short/gaps while
            the bridge stays clean -- and at 1 MHz the 16.5 ms transfer would
            eat most of the ~20 ms A2DP cadence. The smaller 1036-byte frame
            (1024 payload) was clean at 10 MHz, should the ceiling ever drop.

            Lives here (rather than the bridge's own Kconfig) because the symbol
            is shared through this component and both ends print it.

    config DANCEFLOOR_WIFI_LOGS
        bool "Forward logs + telemetry over WiFi to a collector"
        default n
        help
          Ships every ESP_LOG line (MSG_LOG) and a structured HEALTH snapshot
          (MSG_HEALTH) over the sync UDP socket to a laptop collector that has
          joined the AP, for bench analysis. The hub relays its own and every
          satellite's logs to whichever laptop sent MSG_LOG_SUB.

          Off in production. The receive path must never block on a network
          send -- a blocking log write in rx_task starves the UDP mailbox and
          causes the very loss it was logging (satellite/main/main.c:286) --
          so the shipper is a low-priority task fed by a non-blocking,
          drop-on-full queue, and the whole feature compiles out when this is
          unset: zero airtime, zero feedback-loop risk, no wifi_log symbols.

          Enable for bench in the role's sdkconfig.defaults. The bridge is not
          affected: it has no WiFi, and its logs reach the collector over USB.

endmenu
